KNOWNBUG
main.cpp
--cpp11 --unwind 5 --no-unwinding-assertions
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ multi-arg function call: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ WRONG must FAIL: FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
Multi-argument std::function construction and call.  Layer-1 partial-spec pack
deduction (cpp11_partial_spec_pack_after_fixed) and the member function-pointer
pack-arity collapse (cpp11_variadic_pack_in_member_funptr_type) are both fixed,
so the converting constructor now resolves and wires up _M_invoker / _M_manager
with the correct arity.  The remaining gap is the body pack-expansion use: the
operator() body `_M_invoker(_M_functor, std::forward<_ArgTypes>(__args)...)`
expands the parameter pack to a single argument (see
cpp11_variadic_pack_in_method_body_call), so the multi-argument call is rejected
/ mis-evaluated.  Single-argument std::function invokes correctly and soundly
(N5008 [func.wrap.func], [temp.variadic]/5).  Previously this test passed only
VACUOUSLY ("found no match for symbol 'function'").  Flip to CORE once the body
pack-expansion uses the full deduced arity.
